++ and -- must read the variable at ITS width, not the register's.

Both increment paths did

    emit_dereference(REGISTER_ZERO, "Get value");

which always loads a full register -- `ldr x0,[x0]` on aarch64 -- whatever the
variable's type. Every other read in the compiler goes through
load_value(size, is_signed), which takes both from the type.

WHILE `int` WAS EIGHT BYTES THOSE WERE THE SAME THING for the common case, and
that is why this sat unnoticed. At four bytes the read takes the variable AND
the four bytes after it. In tcc's dynarray_add,

    pp[nb++] = data;

nb is 1 and the next slot holds the high half of a stack pointer, so qemu's
register dump at the fault reads

    PC=0x526274  X00=0000400000000001

-- the correct value with an address welded to its top. The store that followed
went through a wild pointer, and the tcc micro-c built segfaulted before it had
parsed anything. Even `--version` died.

HOW IT WAS FOUND, because the route is the lesson. Five rounds of hand-written
reductions all PASSED while the real file faulted; the register dump named it
in one command. `qemu -d cpu,nochain` plus the label map from the .hex2 files
turns "it crashes somewhere" into a corrupted value and the instruction that
produced it. That should have been the first move, not the twelfth.

WHAT THIS FIXES, MEASURED: difftest 83/85 -> 84/85 at four-byte int.
61-prefix-operator-through-a-dot passes. mc-tcc stops dying at startup and gets
as far as emitting a 1,038-byte object.

WHAT IT DOES NOT: 14-function-pointer-member still faults, and mc-tcc still
segfaults later in its run. There is at least one more read of the same shape.

AND IT HAS NO FAILING TEST OF ITS OWN AT EIGHT-BYTE int. Every narrow-type
probe -- char, short and int32_t members, prefix and postfix, with non-zero
neighbouring bytes -- passes without this change, because the array-index path
does not reach emit_dereference. It is landed as correct-in-principle and
demonstrably necessary once int is four bytes, not because a case fails today.

--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -2096,7 +2096,33 @@ void postfix_expr_inc_or_dec(void)
 	emit_push(REGISTER_ONE, "Old register one value");
 
 	emit_push(REGISTER_ZERO, "Address of variable");
-	emit_dereference(REGISTER_ZERO, "Get value");
+	/* AT THE VARIABLE'S WIDTH, NOT THE REGISTER'S.
+	 *
+	 * emit_dereference always loads a full register. While `int` was eight
+	 * bytes that was the same thing; at four it reads the variable AND the
+	 * four bytes after it. In dynarray_add,
+	 *
+	 *     pp[nb++] = data;
+	 *
+	 * nb is 1 and the next slot holds the high half of a stack pointer, so
+	 * the postfix read produced
+	 *
+	 *     X00=0000400000000001
+	 *
+	 * -- the right value with an address welded to its top -- and the
+	 * store that followed went through a wild pointer. tcc segfaulted
+	 * before it had parsed anything; even --version died.
+	 *
+	 * load_value is what every other read already uses, and it takes the
+	 * width and the signedness from the type. */
+	if(NULL != current_target && register_size >= current_target->size)
+	{
+		emit_out(load_value(current_target->size, current_target->is_signed));
+	}
+	else
+	{
+		emit_dereference(REGISTER_ZERO, "Get value");
+	}
 
 	/* We need the address to be at the top of the stack and the value to be below it */
 	emit_pop(REGISTER_ONE, "Address of variable");
@@ -3438,7 +3464,16 @@ void primary_expr(void)
 		emit_push(REGISTER_ONE, "Previous value");
 		emit_push(REGISTER_ZERO, "Address of variable");
 
-		emit_dereference(REGISTER_ZERO, "Deref to get value");
+		/* Same as the postfix site above: the prefix operators read the
